.TH BASTION 8 "2026-11-20" "bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bastion, bastionctl \- web server and reverse proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bastion
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-T ]
.br
.B bastion serve
.I dir
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR n ]
.br
.B bastion proxy
.I url
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR n ]
.br
.B bastion passwd
.RB [ \-m
.IR KiB ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR n ]
.RI [ user ]
.br
.B bastion doctor
.br
.B bastion
.BR \-\-dump\-schema " | " \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.br
.B bastionctl
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bastion
serves files and proxies HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 traffic. It runs in the foreground as a
master process that binds the listening sockets and starts workers, a helper and, when ACME is
configured, a certificate job; each child drops to the configured
.B user
and runs under its own seccomp filter and Landlock rules. A supervisor such as systemd
daemonises it (see
.BR SYSTEMD ).
.PP
.B bastionctl
is the same binary started under that name: a client for the local admin API on its Unix
socket (the
.B admin
block of the configuration).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Configuration file. Default:
.IR /etc/bastion/bastion.conf .
.TP
.B \-t
Check the configuration and exit.
.TP
.B \-T
Check, then print the effective configuration and exit. Values are printed after environment
expansion, so the output can contain secrets.
.TP
.BR serve " \fIdir\fP, " proxy " \fIurl\fP"
Run without a configuration file, serving a directory or proxying to one upstream. The default
port is 8080.
.TP
.B passwd
Print an
.B auth_basic_file
line (Argon2id) for a password read from the terminal.
.TP
.B doctor
Report kernel, CPU, NUMA and NIC settings that affect performance. Reads only.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-schema
Print every configuration directive as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, git revision and the versions of the linked libraries.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR TERM ", " INT
Fast stop.
.TP
.B QUIT
Graceful stop: connections in progress finish within
.BR shutdown_timeout .
.TP
.B HUP
Reload: master re-executes itself in place (same PID) with the new configuration; on any error
the running generation keeps serving.
.TP
.B USR1
Reopen log files.
.TP
.B USR2
Live upgrade to the binary now at the same path: a second master starts beside the old one.
Then
.B WINCH
drains the old workers,
.B QUIT
commits and
.B HUP
rolls back.
.B bastionctl upgrade
runs the same sequence.
.SH SYSTEMD
The packages install
.IR /usr/lib/systemd/system/bastion.service ,
a
.B Type=notify
unit: master reports readiness, reloads and watchdog keep-alives to systemd.
.B systemctl reload bastion
sends HUP. Optional socket activation:
.I /usr/share/doc/bastion/examples/bastion.socket
(copy it to
.IR /etc/systemd/system/ ).
Details are in
.IR /usr/share/doc/bastion/systemd.md .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/bastion/bastion.conf
Configuration; kept on package upgrade.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bastion/html
Default document root of the packaged configuration.
.TP
.I /var/log/bastion
Logs, rotated by
.IR /etc/logrotate.d/bastion .
.TP
.IR /var/lib/bastion ", " /var/cache/bastion ", " /run/bastion
State (ACME account and certificates), cache and runtime directories created by the unit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success, 1 configuration or startup error, 2 usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR systemd.socket (5).
The configuration reference is
.IR /usr/share/doc/bastion/config-reference.md .
